@prefix safety: <http://internal/sustainment/safety#> .
@prefix s3kl:  <http://www.lksoft.com/s3kl#> .
@prefix ps:    <http://internal/sustainment/product#> .
# THE MESH IRI IS `invincible-agent/mesh#`, NOT `internal/mesh#`, AND THE REPO CONTAINS BOTH.
# Written wrong here first, copied from `product_structure_extension.ttl` — a neighbour in this
# same directory that declares `http://internal/mesh#`. The triple parsed, the file loaded, and
# `rdfs:subClassOf mesh:Response` pointed at a class that does not exist, so the three response
# classes below inherited NOTHING and still competed for grounding: an answer the router could
# ground a question on, which is the exact exclusion ADR-0046 says this declaration buys.
#
# Caught by `tests/routing/test_response_shapes_are_not_groundable.py`, which parses the graph and
# follows `rdfs:subClassOf+` — so it sees the resolved IRI rather than the text. An unknown prefix
# passes through VERBATIM: the row registers, reports accepted, and never matches.
@prefix mesh:  <http://invincible-agent/mesh#> .
@prefix owl:   <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:   <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:  <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:   <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix skos:  <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix prov:  <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .

# ============================================================================
# SUSTAINMENT SAFETY VOCABULARY (ADR-0051 §1)
#
# THE THESIS: safety assessment is MIL-STD-882's shape — hazard, severity,
# probability, mitigation, risk acceptance by a named authority — laid over a
# RELIABILITY vocabulary that already exists. S3000L carries the reliability
# half in depth and carries none of the 882 half. So this file mints the 882
# concepts and CITES S3000L for causes rather than re-inventing them.
#
# ── THE ADR-0007 SURVEY, RUN 2026-09-11 AND RECORDED HERE IN FULL ───────────
# Recorded found AND not-found, because a later reader cannot otherwise tell
# *surveyed and absent* from *never surveyed*.
#
# SOURCES SURVEYED, AND HOW — this matters, because the obvious instrument is
# BLIND:
#   * S3000L  <http://www.lksoft.com/s3kl#>  — 761 owl:Class, read FROM SOURCE
#     (semanticstep.org, the URL in the prime manifest).
#   * IOF Core <https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/construct/>
#     — 195 terms, read FROM SOURCE (iofoundry/ontology master Core.rdf).
#
#   NOT surveyed through engine-o's /classes, ON PURPOSE. That endpoint requires
#   `?cls a owl:Class ; rdfs:label ?label` and **S3000L declares ZERO rdfs:label
#   on any of its classes** — so all 761 are invisible to it and a survey run
#   that way returns a confident, uniform "not found". The instrument's own
#   control: /classes returns 100 for SUSTAINMENT, 128 MAINTENANCE, 6
#   DATA_ENGINEERING, 5 PORTFOLIO_PLANNING — so it is not truncating, it simply
#   cannot see an unlabelled class. Filed as a platform defect
#   (docs/plans/s3000l-is-primed-and-invisible.md); it is NOT this file's to fix.
#
# ── NOT MINTED, BECAUSE THE STANDARD ALREADY HAS IT ─────────────────────────
# CAUSE. ADR-0051 §1 listed "its causes" as a concept to express. S3000L owns
# this outright and `safety:Cause` is therefore NOT minted. Confirmed present:
#   s3kl:FailureMode, s3kl:LSAFailureMode, s3kl:TechnicalFailureMode,
#   s3kl:FunctionalFailure, s3kl:FailureModeEffect, s3kl:LocalFailureModeEffect,
#   s3kl:HigherFailureModeEffect, s3kl:FailureModeEffectLevel,
#   s3kl:FailureAnalysisItem, s3kl:FailureModeThresholdDefinition,
#   s3kl:LSACandidateFailureModeAndEffectAnalysisActivity (FMECA),
#   s3kl:FailureRate, s3kl:MeanTimeBetweenFailure, s3kl:FailuresPerOperatingHour
# Minting a parallel cause vocabulary over that is the exact mistake
# product_structure_extension.ttl recorded NOT making — inventing a name and
# silently forfeiting the citation while looking standards-aligned.
#
# ── FOUND, CONSIDERED, AND DELIBERATELY NOT REUSED ──────────────────────────
# Each of these matched a search and is a DIFFERENT CONCEPT. Named here so a
# reader can see it was considered rather than missed:
#   s3kl:HardwarePartHazardousClass      hazardous MATERIAL classification
#                                        (hazmat), not a condition that can
#                                        cause a mishap. Not safety:Hazard.
#   s3kl:FunctionalFailureEffectCriticality,
#   s3kl:TaskPersonnelSafetyCriticality,
#   s3kl:TaskProductIntegrityCriticality criticality of a failure effect or of a
#                                        TASK. Ordinal and adjacent, but not
#                                        882 SEVERITY, which classifies a
#                                        mishap outcome.
#   s3kl:FailureModeDetectionAbilityRating_*Likelihood,
#   s3kl:LSAFailureModeDistributionRating_*Likelihood
#                                        ordinal likelihood scales rating
#                                        DETECTION ABILITY and DISTRIBUTION,
#                                        not mishap probability.
#   s3kl:SpecialEventOccurrenceRating_reasonablyProbable
#                                        an occurrence-probability rating on a
#                                        special event. Closest match found;
#                                        still not a hazard's probability.
#   s3kl:AuthorityToOperate, s3kl:AuthorityDrivenTaskRequirement,
#   s3kl:TaskRequirementAuthoritySourceType, s3kl:SubtaskAcceptanceParameter
#                                        authority to OPERATE and acceptance of
#                                        a SUBTASK. Not the authority that
#                                        accepts residual risk.
#
# ── NOT FOUND IN EITHER SOURCE (the mint list, and its evidence) ────────────
# Searched both sources for each; zero matches unless noted above:
#   mishap · severity · mitigation · corrective · deficiency · discrepancy ·
#   consequence          -> zero matches in S3000L AND zero in IOF Core
#   hazard · risk · probability · critical · safety · accept · authority
#                        -> zero in IOF Core; in S3000L only the adjacent-but-
#                           different terms listed above
# IOF Core scored ZERO on all eleven concepts, read from source, so that
# negative is not a labelling artifact either.
# ============================================================================

safety: a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Sustainment safety assessment (MIL-STD-882 shape)" ;
    rdfs:comment """The 882 half of safety assessment, over S3000L's reliability half.
Causes are s3kl:FailureMode and are NOT re-minted here. Every verdict-shaped property
carries an explicit not-assessed value: a hazard nobody has assessed is not_assessed,
never the bottom of the matrix, which reads as assessed-and-negligible.""" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://www.lksoft.com/s3kl#> .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLASSES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

safety:Hazard a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Hazard" ;
    rdfs:comment """A condition that can cause a mishap. MINTED: S3000L's only hazard-shaped
term is HardwarePartHazardousClass, which classifies hazardous MATERIAL and is a different
concept. Carries severity, probability and a status that includes not_assessed.""" ;
    skos:example "Chafed wiring loom in the aft equipment bay can short the primary bus." .

safety:Mitigation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Mitigation" ;
    rdfs:comment """A control on a hazard, with an owner and a field-verification state.
MINTED: neither source has any mitigation, corrective-action, or hazard-control concept —
S3000L's `control` matches are logistics control numbers and trade-security controls.""" ;
    skos:example "Loom re-routed and clamped per TCTO 12-3; verified on 41 of 44 tails." .

safety:SafetyCriticalItem a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Safety-critical item" ;
    rdfs:comment """A part, procedure or inspection whose failure or omission opens a hazard.
MINTED, though S3000L's TaskPersonnelSafetyCriticality is adjacent: that rates a TASK's
personnel-safety criticality, and this identifies the ITEM whose failure opens a hazard.""" .

safety:WriteUp a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Write-up" ;
    rdfs:comment """A deficiency report, hazard report or mishap narrative as ingested — typed,
not chunked (ADR-0041). MINTED: `deficiency` and `discrepancy` return zero in both sources;
S3000L's only report term is DocumentType_TechnicalReport, a document TYPE.""" .

safety:RiskAssessment a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Risk assessment" ;
    rdfs:comment """A DRAFTED severity/probability statement over a hazard, with its evidence,
its acceptance state, and the authority level its risk level implies. The engine drafts these
and cannot accept them (ADR-0051 §7). acceptance_status is drafted | accepted | rejected and is
never defaulted.""" .

safety:AcceptanceAuthority a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Acceptance authority" ;
    rdfs:comment """The ROLE that may accept residual risk at a given level — never a person.
MINTED: S3000L's AuthorityToOperate is authority to OPERATE and SubtaskAcceptanceParameter is
acceptance of a subtask; neither is the authority that takes on residual risk.""" .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RESPONSE CLASSES — every verb's `output_uri`, DECLARED HERE BEFORE ANYTHING
# REGISTERS.
#
# ADDED 2026-09-12, AND THE OMISSION WAS THE EXACT DEFECT THIS ADR OPENS WITH.
# `engine-safety` registered two verbs in `eecb7c4` naming `safety:OrphanedHazardSet`
# and `safety:DeferralRiskCard` as their outputs, and NO TTL declared either. That is
# `mesh:proposeDisposition`'s failure repeated: it named its output class from the day
# it woke, the class existed only because a sandbox seeding script MERGEd it into being,
# and every fresh cluster's registrar — which MATCHes, correctly, and must never invent
# ontology — refused the edge with a Contract D 422 telling the exact truth. Nine of
# Engine A's ten verbs registered and that one could not, permanently.
#
# Caught here by running this ADR's own seal 1 against this engine rather than by a
# cluster refusing it later. `tests/safety/test_output_classes_are_declared.py` now
# DERIVES the population from VERBS, so a verb added without its class goes red offline.
#
# subClassOf mesh:Response to inherit the grounding-pool exclusion (ADR-0046) — a
# response is an answer, and an answer must not become a subject the router grounds on.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Output of `findOrphanedHazards`. THE VERB NAME IS IN A `#` COMMENT RATHER THAN IN THE
# DEFINITION, and that is not tidiness. A definition is RETRIEVAL INPUT: Weaviate embeds
# "<label> — <definition>" and scores a user's query against that text, so a definition naming
# another class competes for that class's traffic — how `idp:Pipeline` won "list the datasets in
# publog", by naming `idp:Dataset`.
#
# The trap here is subtler than naming a sibling in prose. `findOrphanedHazards` is camelCase and
# contains the substring `Hazard`, which is `safety:Hazard`'s OWN LABEL — so citing the verb put
# the subject class's name into the response class's embedded text. Lowercase "hazards" would not
# have done it; the capital did. Caught by `test_no_new_sibling_name_bleed_in_definitions`, and it
# is the second defect in this file this week after the `mesh:` prefix.
safety:OrphanedHazardSet a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf mesh:Response ;
    rdfs:label "Orphaned Hazard Set" ;
    rdfs:comment """Live safety conditions with no owned, field-verified mitigation, ranked by
severity then age, each carrying the mechanism that left it unattended: none recorded, one recorded
with no owner, or an owner whose work nobody has confirmed in the field. Conditions nobody has yet
assessed are reported separately and never counted among them.""" .

safety:DeferralRiskCard a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf mesh:Response ;
    rdfs:label "Deferral Risk Card" ;
    rdfs:comment """Output of assessDeferralRisk: whether a deferred work order's item is
safety-critical, which hazard the deferral re-opens, and the drafted severity and probability
carried from that hazard. Resolves no risk level and no acceptance.""" .

safety:RiskAssessmentDraft a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf mesh:Response ;
    rdfs:label "Risk Assessment Draft" ;
    rdfs:comment """Output of draftRiskAssessment: a DRAFTED severity/probability statement over a
hazard, with a citation for every figure, the risk level resolved from the ratified matrix, and the
acceptance audience that level implies. Carries acceptance_status `drafted` and nothing else — the
engine cannot write `accepted` (ADR-0051 §7), which is a HumanTask disposition by an entitled
authority.""" .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RELATIONSHIPS — the cause side points OUT of this file, on purpose
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

safety:hasCause a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has cause" ;
    rdfs:domain safety:Hazard ;
    rdfs:range s3kl:FailureMode ;
    rdfs:comment """THE CITATION THAT MADE THE SURVEY WORTH RUNNING. A hazard's cause is an
S3000L failure mode, not a minted class. The range is deliberately the standard's own term.""" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom s3kl:FailureMode .

safety:mitigatedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "mitigated by" ;
    rdfs:domain safety:Hazard ;
    rdfs:range safety:Mitigation .

safety:opensHazard a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "opens hazard" ;
    rdfs:domain safety:SafetyCriticalItem ;
    rdfs:range safety:Hazard .

safety:reportsHazard a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "reports hazard" ;
    rdfs:domain safety:WriteUp ;
    rdfs:range safety:Hazard .

safety:assesses a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "assesses" ;
    rdfs:domain safety:RiskAssessment ;
    rdfs:range safety:Hazard .

safety:criticalItemFor a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "critical item for" ;
    rdfs:domain safety:SafetyCriticalItem ;
    rdfs:range ps:Part ;
    rdfs:comment "Binds the safety plane to the canonical product structure rather than to a second parts vocabulary." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ps:Part .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATA PROPERTIES — every verdict-shaped one has an explicit not-assessed value
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

safety:hazardStatus a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "hazard status" ;
    rdfs:domain safety:Hazard ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment """open | mitigated | closed | not_assessed. NO DEFAULT. A hazard nobody has
assessed is not_assessed; defaulting it to anything else is the optimistic-default dishonesty
the `favourable` ruling already refused one domain over.""" .

safety:verifiedInField a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "verified in field" ;
    rdfs:domain safety:Mitigation ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment """true | false | not_verified. Three-state for the same reason: a mitigation
closed on paper and never verified in the field is the second of ADR-0051's four failures, and
a boolean cannot say it.""" .

safety:acceptanceStatus a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "acceptance status" ;
    rdfs:domain safety:RiskAssessment ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment """drafted | accepted | rejected. The engine writes only `drafted`; the other
two are HumanTask dispositions by an entitled authority (ADR-0051 §5) and no verb may set
them.""" .

safety:severity a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "severity" ;
    rdfs:domain safety:Hazard ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment "Validated against safety_risk_matrix.ttl. Unrecognized values are a loud refusal, never coerced." .

safety:probability a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "probability" ;
    rdfs:domain safety:Hazard ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment "Validated against safety_risk_matrix.ttl. Unrecognized values are a loud refusal, never coerced." .

safety:acceptanceAuthorityLevel a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "acceptance authority level" ;
    rdfs:domain safety:RiskAssessment ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment "The level the drafted risk IMPLIES, resolved from the matrix. Records what the draft would require, not what anyone granted." .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PROVENANCE PREDICATES ON THE MATRIX ITSELF — declared 2026-09-14, and the reason
# they are being declared NOW is the finding.
#
# `safety:derivedFromStandard` and `safety:ratificationStatus` have been in USE on
# `safety_risk_matrix.ttl` since it was written, and were DECLARED NOWHERE. The file
# parsed, loaded and primed exactly as if they were — an undeclared predicate is not
# an error in RDF, it is a triple whose subject and object are fine and whose meaning
# is nobody's.
#
# THAT IS THE THIRD TIME THIS EXACT SHAPE HAS APPEARED IN THIS ARC, and it is why the
# declaration is here rather than a note saying it should be: a `mesh:` prefix bound to
# a class that did not exist, a docs citation naming a packet on another branch, and a
# decision row naming a definition never authored. Each one a reference that LOOKS
# satisfied because nothing followed it. Adding a THIRD undeclared predicate while
# reading a standard from source to fix a provenance gap would have been the same
# error committed inside its own correction.
#
# The pattern was inherited honestly — `pcn_disposition_rules.ttl` uses
# `pcn:ratificationStatus` undeclared too, and this file's matrix was built to match
# its neighbour. That is how a convention spreads without anyone choosing it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

safety:derivedFromStandard a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "derived from standard" ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment """The STANDARD a policy table was transcribed from, named to the section.
Deliberately an annotation on the table rather than a value on each row: the citation is a fact
about the whole transcription, and per-row copies would drift against each other.

Kept SEPARATE from safety:ladderDerivedFromInstruction below. The matrix comes from MIL-STD-882E
and the acceptance ladder from a DoD instruction; one predicate covering both would make a later
change to either one invisible, which is exactly the state the ladder spent two days in.""" .

safety:ladderDerivedFromInstruction a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "ladder derived from instruction" ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment """The INSTRUCTION that names the risk acceptance authority per level, and the
concurrence that precedes the Serious and High ones. 882E 4.3.7 defers this question by name —
"accepted by the appropriate authority as defined in DoDI 5000.02" — so the ladder can never
carry the matrix's citation, and this predicate exists so it cannot accidentally be given one.

DoDI 5000.88 PARA 3.6.e.(1)(b)1 as of 2026-09-14. The instruction has moved between issues once
already (the 2020 reissue pulled engineering content out of 5000.02), which is the argument for
recording WHICH issue rather than only which paragraph.""" .

safety:ratificationStatus a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "ratification status" ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment """Who, if anyone, must sign this table off — and it is a STATUS rather than a
signature. A table matching the standard needs no local ratifier because it has one already; a
programme's TAILORING needs the SSPP signatory. The distinction is the whole reason the field
exists: without it, "unratified" reads as a defect on a table that is correctly citing a standard.

Mirrors pcn:ratificationStatus on the disposition rules by intent, not by import — a shared
spelling across two domain ontologies is a convention, and neither should silently constrain the
other's vocabulary.""" .

safety:acceptanceAuthorityTier a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "acceptance authority tier" ;
    rdfs:domain safety:RiskLevel ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment """CAE_OR_DAE | PEO_LEVEL | PM. The OFFICE DoDI 5000.88 PARA 3.6.e.(1)(b)1
assigns each risk level, held separately from `safety:acceptanceAudience` because they answer
different questions: the audience is which queue the acceptance lands in HERE, the tier is what
the instruction REQUIRES. A deployment re-points the audience constantly and the tier almost
never, and collapsing them would make a tailoring indistinguishable from a transcription error.

"CAE_OR_DAE" and "PEO_LEVEL" are spelled as the instruction spells them. The High authority may
be the Defense Acquisition Executive rather than the Component one, and "program executive
officer-level" is a LEVEL rather than the PEO in person — reading either as a single named post
would make the seed STRICTER than its source, which fails by blocking a lawful acceptance and is
the direction nobody files a defect about.""" .

safety:authorityRank a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "authority rank" ;
    rdfs:domain safety:RiskLevel ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:comment """Seniority order of the acceptance authority, 1 most senior. Exists for ONE
purpose: the DIRECTIONAL half of the ladder seal, which asserts that no level names a more junior
office than the instruction requires.

A plain equality check catches any difference; the directional assertion is what names the
DANGEROUS difference, and the two are stated separately for the same reason the matrix states
them separately — an equality failure says "the table changed", and a directional failure says
"a High risk is now signable by a program manager", which are not the same report.""" .
